> "The most expensive capital mistake isn't rejection — it's misalignment."

## Michael's Memo: Credit Education for Visionary Builders

March is National Credit Education Month.

But for business owners, "credit education" isn't about FICO scores.

It's about understanding:

- When to pursue bank or alternative financing
- When structure matters more than rate
- How lien position impacts flexibility
- Where alternative lenders align best for growth seasons

We frequently meet profitable, multi-million-dollar companies that don't need *more capital*.

They need the right structure.

An ABL instead of a term loan.

A revolver instead of stacked advances.

A bridge instead of long-term debt.

Capital education is knowing the difference.

Smart capital isn't just about access. It's about alignment with the stage your business is in today, and where you want to take it next. That's true whether you own the business or advise one.

Here to serve,

Michael Kodinsky, Founder & CEO

## Deal Highlight: $1.5MM Asset-Based Revolver for an Established Roofing Contractor

A 38-year-old commercial roofing contractor, acquired by private equity 15 months earlier, closed a record revenue year with a net loss and needed liquidity to pre-purchase materials, smooth receivable cycles, and take on larger commercial contracts. Serve Funding arranged a $1,500,000 asset-based revolving line of credit: 85% advance on eligible receivables, Prime + 3%, a 24-month facility with a 90-day exit option, and no unused line fee.

The full story, including the bid-pricing trend that nearly killed the deal and how the lender met the client on it, is in the case study: [$1.5MM ABL for a PE-Backed Roofer](/blog/roofing-contractor-abl-bid-pricing-lesson).

{% callout type="tip" title="Why this capital was aligned" %}
When seasoned contractors enter acquisition-driven expansion, capital timing becomes strategic. Sometimes the difference between financial pressure and growth momentum is simply the right financing structure.
{% /callout %}

## More Than Money: Core Value, Service

At Serve Funding, **Service** isn't just something we say. It's how we operate.

When a business owner reaches out to us, it usually means they're navigating an important moment:

- A growth opportunity
- A cash flow challenge
- An acquisition or transition

Our role is to step in as an advisor who listens carefully, understands the full picture, and helps design capital solutions that truly support the business.

Service means:

- Taking the time to understand your goals
- Structuring capital responsibly
- Communicating clearly throughout the process
- Staying focused on long-term success, not just a quick transaction

Because when businesses grow sustainably, everyone wins.

And that's the outcome we're here to serve.
